{"alias":"con_0c9b0ea819584e198a9de036ee8ebd6f","dates":{},"display_label":"Math-3471-A1","record":{"artifacts":[{"location":"in_repository","media_type":"text/markdown","path":"files/attempt.md","role":"manuscript"},{"location":"in_repository","media_type":"text/markdown","path":"files/disclosures.md","role":"supplementary"}],"attribution":{"contribution_mode":"ai_only","humans":[{"authorship_confirmation":"confirmed","display_name":"gavenren","github_id":206281466,"login_at_event":"gavenren","person_ref":"per_4f262210ec784630b1f008b4f00640d8","roles":["prompting","submission"]}],"organisations":[],"submitter":{"authorship_confirmation":"confirmed","display_name":"gavenren","github_id":206281466,"login_at_event":"gavenren","person_ref":"per_4f262210ec784630b1f008b4f00640d8","roles":["prompting","submission"]}},"claim":{"assumptions":[],"limitations":["Full disproof applies to the prior d>=1 catalogue statement, not the corrected k,n,d>=2 conjecture."],"scope_statement":"Disproves the catalogue formula as explicitly quantified for all n,d>=1 and k>=2 over C: at (k,d,n)=(4,1,3) it predicts 5, whereas the generic fourth-power rank of ternary quartics is 6. Supplies a self-contained catalecticant lower bound and surjective-Jacobian/constructibility upper bound, plus exact integer matrices and two-algorithm determinant certificates. This is a correction of the catalogue scope using a classical Alexander–Hirschowitz exception, not a newly discovered result. The original 2017 conjecture explicitly restricts k,n,d>=2; that higher-degree conjecture is not refuted or resolved here. No claim is made about every quartic, real typical ranks, or all other parameter triples.","type":"full_disproof"},"contribution_id":"con_0c9b0ea819584e198a9de036ee8ebd6f","declarations":{"ai_disclosure":{"actor":"per_4f262210ec784630b1f008b4f00640d8","confirmed":true,"evidence_url":"https://github.com/We-Solve/research/issues/30","recorded_at":"2026-10-18T16:25:49Z","source":"form_field"},"public